# On x86-64 an entrypoint that is not hot needs no frame to write down which function object it was entered through: the stub is told the stack pointer of the caller, and nobody walks the stack below it. An entrypoint that does nothing else is no function of the image: its few bytes lie in front of the function, as those of an entrypoint that only moves a register. No table says where they begin: whoever asks finds the function, which is the same one as it is written.
# FORM: AsRead
# RUN: generate-code calls FunctionsWithoutFrame
# REQUIRES: x86_64 unsanitized-code
# RUN(IMAGE): compile JumpOnlyFunctionsRemoved ImageFunctionsEmitted InnerEntryBytesEmitted
# REQUIRES(IMAGE): x86_64 unsanitized-code
# OPTIONS: heat=OnlyLoopsAreHot disable=pass:InlineCalls,pass:OutlineColdOperations
# CHECK: f7: size={{*}} frame=none entries=1 sites=0
# CHECK-NEXT: function f2 x1
# CHECK-NEXT: stub RecordCallee x1
# CHECK: statistic FunctionsWithoutFrame 5
# IMAGE: statistic JumpOnlyFunctionsRemoved 4
# IMAGE: statistic ImageFunctionsEmitted 5
# IMAGE: statistic InnerEntryBytesEmitted 0
# PROGRAM-OF: entrypoints-of-closures-that-leave-sight-record-what-they-were-entered-through.ir
